# Empty Interface in Go

`interface{}` (or `any` since Go 1.18) is an interface that defines no methods. Since every type satisfies an empty interface automatically, `any` can store a **value of any type**.

## `interface{}` and `any`

```go
// Since Go 1.18, any is an alias for interface{}
var x any

x = 42
fmt.Println(x)    // 42
x = "hello"
fmt.Println(x)    // hello
x = []int{1,2,3}
fmt.Println(x)    // [1 2 3]
```

## Why `any` Exists

```go
// fmt.Println accepts anything
func Println(a ...any) (n int, err error)

// Heterogeneous slice
data := []any{1, "two", 3.0, true}
for _, v := range data {
    fmt.Printf("%T: %v\n", v, v)
}
```

## Dangers: Loss of Type Safety

```go
var x any = 42
// x + 1 // ERROR: cannot operate on any

// Must type assert before using
if n, ok := x.(int); ok {
    fmt.Println(n + 1) // 43
}
```

## When to Use `any`

- Use when truly heterogeneous data is needed
- Avoid when you can use Go generics (1.18+) or a specific interface
- Signals loss of compile-time type safety

starterCode:
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"reflect"
)

// Generic cache using any
type Cache struct {
	data map[string]any
}

func NewCache() *Cache {
	return &Cache{data: make(map[string]any)}
}

func (c *Cache) Set(key string, value any) {
	c.data[key] = value
}

func (c *Cache) Get(key string) (any, bool) {
	v, ok := c.data[key]
	return v, ok
}

// Function accepting any type
func describe(v any) {
	t := reflect.TypeOf(v)
	if t == nil {
		fmt.Println("nil")
		return
	}
	fmt.Printf("Type: %-12s Kind: %-10s Value: %v\n", t.Name(), t.Kind(), v)
}

// Type-safe add using any (not recommended — shows the problem)
func addAny(a, b any) any {
	switch v := a.(type) {
	case int:
		if w, ok := b.(int); ok {
			return v + w
		}
	case float64:
		if w, ok := b.(float64); ok {
			return v + w
		}
	case string:
		if w, ok := b.(string); ok {
			return v + w
		}
	}
	return nil
}

func main() {
	// Store different types
	var x any
	values := []any{42, "hello", 3.14, true, []int{1, 2, 3}}
	for _, v := range values {
		x = v
		describe(x)
	}

	// Cache example
	cache := NewCache()
	cache.Set("user_id", 123)
	cache.Set("username", "alice")
	cache.Set("scores", []int{90, 85, 92})

	if v, ok := cache.Get("username"); ok {
		fmt.Printf("\nCached username: %v (type: %T)\n", v, v)
	}

	// addAny example
	fmt.Println("\naddAny:", addAny(10, 20))
	fmt.Println("addAny:", addAny("foo", "bar"))
	fmt.Println("addAny mismatch:", addAny(10, "bar")) // nil
}
